<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonsine Tables</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1252–1270</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alfonsine Tables were astronomical tables for calculating the positions of the Sun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a> and planets, compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> by the Jewish scholars Isaac ibn Sid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> versions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and first printed in 1483, they remained the standard tables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until the Copernican Prutenic Tables of 1551.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonsine-tables.jpg" alt="Alfonsine Tables" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablas_alfonsies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
